module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_bare_negative_integer_literal_in_a_claim_body_loses_its_verdict

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_bare_negative_integer_literal_in_a_claim_body_loses_its_verdict: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_bare_negative_integer_literal_in_a_claim_body_loses_its_verdict" as NonEmptyStr,
  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a test-fn claim body whose comparison operand is a bare negative integer literal -- the unary-minus spelling `== -1` / `== -2` rather than the corpus spelling `== 0 - 1` / `== 0 - 2` -- is planned by the floor and never reaches a terminal verdict. The witness neither passes nor fails; it sits between the two, and the floor's changed-witness gate is what eventually notices.",
    "HARM: the failure surfaces as an engine-sounding verdict (changed_witness_planned_without_terminal_verdict) that points at the changed-witness roster rather than at the authoring spelling, so the first diagnosis is in the wrong module. Nothing in the parse, the plan, or the per-witness receipt names the literal. A claim that can never answer is worse than a red one: the red is loud where it stands, while this one is silent inside a gate whose job is to catch something else, and the author's natural spelling is the trap.",
    "RECEIPT, AND IT IS A LIVE ONE RATHER THAN A CONSTRUCTED SPECIMEN: PR gunbc#13257 at head 74eec694d0, floor run 37207266602 job 111451921277. dag/test/claim/fabric/systemd_run_transient_wait_witness_test.dag carried the one-hot cardinality probe family over `materialized_cardinality`; exactly two members -- probe_materialized_cardinality_refused and probe_materialized_cardinality_command_refused, the two whose bodies compare `== -1` and `== -2` -- were reported as identity=<probe> standing=planned-without-terminal-verdict disposition=planned_as_changed_witness outcome=failed cause=changed_witness_planned_without_terminal_verdict. The four siblings comparing against non-negative literals all reached terminal verdicts in the same run, including probe_materialized_cardinality_11, which evaluates through the same helper whose match arms contain the same negative sentinels.",
    "THE INTERVENTION AND WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT PROVE: rewriting the two comparisons in place to `== 0 - 1` and `== 0 - 2` (commit dc666b6547 on the same branch) restored terminal verdicts for all fifteen witnesses of that entry in the very next run -- ten passes and five designed fails, every one answered. That is a real paired observation in one entry, but it does NOT isolate the spelling as the whole boundary, and this row refuses to overclaim it: the corpus on origin/main at filing carries 139 occurrences of the unary spelling (`git grep -E \"(==|=>) -[0-9]\"` over dag/ and src/v2/), including claim modules that reach terminal verdicts on green main -- dag/test/claim/bmc/bmc_onboarding_lifecycle_witness_test.dag compares `next_tag(p: FabricJoined) == -1` inside a Bool claim body and answers. So the class is narrower than the spelling: some gate -- engine version, module context, or evaluator route -- lets the same spelling answer elsewhere while refusing it in the specimen. The boundary is real but unisolated, and the `0 - N` spelling is the standing safe one the corpus already uses everywhere it must answer.",
    "POPULATION, BY INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN BY ASSERTION: the grep above is the census at filing grain -- 139 occurrences of the unary spelling across dag/ and src/v2/, of which the specimen's two were the only ones observed losing a verdict. A transcribed count is not the authority and would rot; the census command is the reproduction route, and a future run that reclassifies any of the 139 into this row's standing is how the population grows.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: mechanically preventable, and BY ACCIDENT, twice over. Nothing in the parse, normalize, resolve, or claim-body lowering refuses the spelling, so no wall exists; what prevents it in practice is the corpus convention -- authors copy `0 - N` from neighbouring claim bodies and never write the unary form. A convention is not a wall: it guards only the modules whose authors copied correctly, which is exactly the coverage shape this ledger's rung vocabulary calls accidental.",
    "CEILING: structurally impossible, once the boundary is isolated and walled. The class is decidable at the literal-to-term boundary: a lowering that treats a unary-minus integer literal as `0 - N` leaves no accepted program in the class, and a lowering that refuses the spelling with a typed located diagnostic naming the span also leaves none. Either wall makes the invalid state unconstructible, so section 4b admits nothing weaker -- but neither wall can be PLACED honestly until the paired observation isolates which gate the specimen tripped, and a wall placed on an unisolated boundary would be sized to a guess.",
    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY AND NOT AN ARTIFACT: the isolation experiment first -- one module, one Int helper returning a negative sentinel, two test fns differing in exactly one term (`== -1` versus `== 0 - 1`), run under the floor's own pinned engine rather than a session-built one, with the verdict-less arm's lowering trace captured. Then the wall: the claim-body lowering either lowers a unary-minus integer literal to the term `0 - N` lowers to, or refuses it at declaration with a typed located diagnostic naming the span, executed on that paired probe -- the refused arm must lose its verdict NO MORE (it must be refused, not planned), and the `0 - N` arm must evaluate unchanged. Owner unassigned at filing. This row is NOT retired by the corpus convention, by a session-local workaround, or by the specimen's own fix.",
    "DISPOSITION: filed from gunbc#13257, the debt-paydown trial's typed-argv cutover, whose floor run is the receipt. #13257 carried the corpus `0 - N` spelling as its fix and deliberately does NOT build the lowering wall -- that is this row's trigger, not that PR's scope. That PR has since restructured the specimen family: the five designed-fail one-hot probes were rewritten as TRUE-RETURNING negative controls -- each control asserts its own discrimination as a conjunction (cardinality != 0 && != 1 && != 10, the refused arm is not produced), so the absence claims go red rather than silent and no designed-false claim remains in the tree; the family holds 12/12 terminal PASS over its entry (9 real witnesses + 3 controls) and is NOT enrolled expected-red because nothing is red. See that PR's description for the restructure argument.",
  ],
  evidence: [],
}
